  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong cold front brings gusty winds & potential for severe \nthunderstorms on Friday.\n\nSome light rain or drizzle will be possible tonight through\nabout 07Z or 08Z before it exits out. Low cigs will be the main\nconcern overnight.\n\nTomorrow, a brief period of warm temperatures and sunny \nconditions returns as southwest winds gradually increase \nthroughout the day. Meanwhile, a deep digging trough forming \nover the Midwest will quickly traverse the Ohio River Valley \nbefore tracking towards the East Coast, forming a line of \nthunderstorms that will move through in the afternoon & evening \ntimeframe. Combined with warmer temperatures (with highs in the \n80s by Friday afternoon) and low-end instability, some of these \nthunderstorms could become severe as the associated cold front \ntracks through the region. Due to conditions remaining \nrelatively dry (ensemble guidance indicates PWATs ranging from \n1.2-1.4 inches), the primary hazard will likely be gusty to \ndamaging winds as DCAPE ranges in the low-1000s and shear \nremains elevated. SPC has included the metro regions in a Slight\nrisk for severe weather impacts during this time period. While \ndamaging wind gusts are going to be the primary threat, a quick \nQLCS tornado spin- up is certainly possible. \n\nThis line of thunderstorms pushes east of the area by late Friday \nnight, but the trough will lag behind this line of thunderstorms and \ntraverse the area throughout the day Saturday. Thus, additional rain \nshowers remain possible into the weekend as this trough finally \npushes through. While winds likely remain elevated through this \nperiod, with peak gusts over the mountains approaching 30-40 MPH, \nmost areas likely stay below wind advisory criteria. Conditions \nbegin to clear by Sunday, with much colder temperatures settling in \nin the wake of the front. Highs on Saturday only reach the\nmid-50s as the trough moves through, with continued cooler\ntemperatures anticipated through the start of next week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures Sunday through Wednesday. Possible \nfrost and freezing temperatures in the Appalachians early Sunday. \nDry each period.\n\nA sharp transition to cold weather is expected behind a strong cold \nfront this weekend. This dry airmass in combination with clearing \nskies and weakening winds creates an ideal environment for \nefficient radiational cooling conditions throughout the area. \nOvernight temperatures could drop to near or below freezing west\nof the Blue Ridge on Saturday and Sunday night. Most areas \nacross our region have opportunities for frost to form, \nparticularly west of I-95.\n\nHigh pressure will build across the region Sunday through Wednesday.\nLight and variable winds in the mountains of the Appalachians could \nresult in areas of frost and near freezing temperatures Sunday \nmorning. Elsewhere and otherwise, dry conditions Sunday through \nWednesday in part to the area of high pressure. Temperatures will \nmodify some toward late Tuesday into Wednesday.",
